// End-to-end check for the X11 hotkey delivery path.
//
// what: register a hotkey on the X11 input path and prove that a key
//   event for it actually arrives and fires the binding - and that the
//   modifier on the binding is the one that is matched.
// why: the X11 root-window subscription could be REFUSED by the X server
//   (BadAccess) - and because havel asked for the key events and the
//   pointer events in ONE XSelectInput call, the refused pointer bits took
//   the key events down with them. The process then received NO key events
//   at all, for its whole lifetime, and every X11 hotkey was dead.
//
//   A separate defect swapped Ctrl and Shift in the match on the X11 path,
//   so a Ctrl hotkey was matched as if it required Shift.
//
// expected: PASS - the Ctrl binding fires exactly once for a Ctrl+F24
//   press, and the Shift binding does not fire for that press.
//
//   Note on the plain binding: a hotkey with no modifier matches whatever
//   modifiers happen to be held (the "ignore modifiers unless explicitly
//   set" rule), so the plain F24 binding fires for a Ctrl+F24 press too.
//   That is the documented rule, not a defect - which is why the
//   discrimination check here uses a DIFFERENT modifier (Shift) instead.
//
// run: ./build-debug/havel --io x11 scripts/tests/test_x11_hotkey_delivery.hv
//   Manual-run: the script registers a real hotkey and a helper process
//   injects the key. It is NOT in scripts/smoke - injecting real input
//   must never happen in the automated suite.
//
// The injector (scripts/tests/x11inject.c) uses XTest to synthesize the
// key press, so no physical input is involved. Build it first:
//   clang -O0 scripts/tests/x11inject.c -o /tmp/opencode/x11inject -lX11 -lXtst

firedCtrl = 0
firedShift = 0

// The Ctrl modifier is the point of the test: before the layout fix, a
// Ctrl hotkey was matched as if it required Shift.
//
// '^' is the Ctrl shorthand and '+' is the Shift shorthand, so '^F24' is
// Ctrl+F24 and '^+F24' would be Ctrl+Shift+F24.
^F24 => {
    firedCtrl = firedCtrl + 1
    print("CTRL_FIRED")
}

// A Shift binding: with the Ctrl/Shift bits swapped, this one would have
// matched the Ctrl press instead of the Ctrl one.
+F24 => {
    firedShift = firedShift + 1
    print("SHIFT_FIRED")
}

// The hotkeys are registered when the script's top level runs, but the
// X11 adapter needs its own subscription to be in place before a key event
// can arrive. Give it a moment, then inject the key from a helper process:
// the event is synthesized by the X server, so it travels the exact same
// path as a real key press.
sleep(1200)

injector = "/tmp/opencode/x11inject"
if !fs.exists(injector) {
    print("FAIL: injector missing - build scripts/tests/x11inject.c first")
    exit(1)
}

r = process.pipe([injector, "F24", "ctrl"])
if !r.success {
    print("FAIL: injector failed: " + str(r.error))
    exit(1)
}

// Give the event time to travel from the X server to the binding.
sleep(1200)

if firedCtrl != 1 {
    print("FAIL: Ctrl+F24 fired " + str(firedCtrl) + " times, expected 1")
    exit(1)
}
if firedShift != 0 {
    print("FAIL: Shift+F24 fired " + str(firedShift) + " times for a Ctrl press")
    exit(1)
}
print("PASS: X11 hotkey delivery works and Ctrl is not matched as Shift")

// The registered hotkeys keep the event loop alive, so the process would
// otherwise linger forever. exit(0) ends it with the assertions above
// already evaluated.
exit(0)
